{"name":"Buoyant Blue","hex":"#84addb","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"buoyant","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having buoyancy; able to float.","Lighthearted and lively."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/buoyant"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Buoyancy","blocks":[{"text":"Buoyancy, or upthrust, is the force exerted by a fluid opposing the weight of a partially or fully immersed object. The pressure of a fluid increases with depth below the fluid's surface because of the weight of the fluid above. Thus, the pressure at the bottom of a container of fluid is greater than at the top of the container. Similarly, the pressure at the bottom of an object submerged in a fluid is greater than at the top of the object. This pressure difference results in a net upward force on the object. The magnitude of the force is proportional to the pressure difference, and, as explained by Archimedes' principle, is equivalent to the weight of the fluid that would otherwise occupy the submerged volume of the object, i.e. the displaced fluid."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Buoyancy.svg/330px-Buoyancy.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buoyancy"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Pound-coin-floating-in-mercury.jpg/500px-Pound-coin-floating-in-mercury.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Pound-coin-floating-in-mercury.jpg/1280px-Pound-coin-floating-in-mercury.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Pound-coin-floating-in-mercury.jpg/500px-Pound-coin-floating-in-mercury.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A metallic coin (an old British pound coin) floats in mercury due to the buoyancy force upon it and appears to float higher because of the surface tension of the mercury.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APound-coin-floating-in-mercury.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Canard_Colvert_02.jpg/500px-Canard_Colvert_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Canard_Colvert_02.jpg/1280px-Canard_Colvert_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Canard_Colvert_02.jpg/500px-Canard_Colvert_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A duck has difficulties to get under water due to its buoyancy. When no swimming forces are implied, the natural equilibrium of forces keeps about half of the duck above water. ","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACanard_Colvert_02.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Pressure_distribution_on_an_immersed_cube.png/500px-Pressure_distribution_on_an_immersed_cube.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Pressure_distribution_on_an_immersed_cube.png/1280px-Pressure_distribution_on_an_immersed_cube.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Pressure_distribution_on_an_immersed_cube.png/500px-Pressure_distribution_on_an_immersed_cube.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pressure distribution on an immersed cube","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APressure_distribution_on_an_immersed_cube.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Forces_on_an_immersed_cube.png/500px-Forces_on_an_immersed_cube.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Forces_on_an_immersed_cube.png/1280px-Forces_on_an_immersed_cube.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Forces_on_an_immersed_cube.png/500px-Forces_on_an_immersed_cube.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Forces on an immersed cube","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AForces_on_an_immersed_cube.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wiki